-- Database Management System | ESE | medium | TestId 4400093523020129 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (4400093523020129, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A practical problem in Database Management System requires the behaviour or method described by the syllabus area concerned with Introducing the database and DBMS. Explain how Introducing the database and DBMS applies, outline the main steps and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical problem in Database Management System requires the behaviour or method described by the syllabus area concerned with Structure of relational databases. Explain how Structure of relational databases applies, outline the main steps and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical problem in Database Management System requires the behaviour or method described by entities, their attributes and relationships are represented before making relational tables. Explain how Basic Entity Relationship Concepts: Entities applies, outline the main steps and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical problem in Database Management System requires the behaviour or method described by table design is refined to reduce avoidable redundancy and modification anomalies. Explain how Normalization of database tables: Database Tables and Normalization applies, outline the main steps and justify the result."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Introducing the database and DBMS is covered in the syllabus unit “Database Systems Data Models”. A complete answer should define the concept, describe its role and steps/components, relate it to Files and File Systems, Problems with File System and advantages of Database Management systems, The importance of Data models, Data Model Basic Building Blocks, provide a practical example, and state a relevant check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Files and File Systems; Problems with File System and advantages of Database Management systems; The importance of Data models; Data Model Basic Building Blocks. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Database Management System task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Structure of relational databases is covered in the syllabus unit “Relational Data Model”. A complete answer should define the concept, describe its role and steps/components, relate it to Domains, Relations, Relational algebra, Relational algebra-Primary Operators, provide a practical example, and state a relevant check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Domains; Relations; Relational algebra; Relational algebra-Primary Operators. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Database Management System task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** An ER model describes entities, attributes and relationships; cardinality and participation constraints inform conversion into a relational schema.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Relationship; Attributes; E R Diagram symbols; Conversion of Entity Relationship Model into Relations. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Database Management System task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Normalization organizes relational tables to reduce redundancy and insertion, update and deletion anomalies using dependency-based rules.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as The need for Normalization; The Normal forms:1NF; The Normal form: 3NF; The Normal forms: BCNF. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Database Management System task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

