VALUES (440093201301015, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A practical problem in Digital Electronics requires the behaviour or method described by the syllabus area concerned with Digital and Analog systems. Explain how Digital and Analog systems applies, outline the main steps and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical problem in Digital Electronics requires the behaviour or method described by values are represented in base two using powers of two as place weights. Explain how Number System: Binary number system applies, outline the main steps and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical problem in Digital Electronics requires the behaviour or method described by the syllabus area concerned with Logic Gates: Positive and Negative logic. Explain how Logic Gates: Positive and Negative logic applies, outline the main steps and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical problem in Digital Electronics requires the behaviour or method described by the syllabus area concerned with logic level and pulse waveforms. Explain how logic level and pulse waveforms applies, outline the main steps and justify the result."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Digital and Analog systems is covered in the syllabus unit “Module-1 Introduction”. A complete answer should define the concept, describe its role and steps/components, relate it to logic level and pulse waveforms, elements of digital logic, Functions of digital logic, Module-1 Introduction, provide a practical example, and state a relevant check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as logic level and pulse waveforms; elements of digital logic; Functions of digital logic; Module-1 Introduction. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Digital Electronics task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Binary is base 2 and uses powers of two as place values; conversion follows the weight of each bit.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as (addition; subtraction; multiplication; Division). State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Digital Electronics task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Logic Gates: Positive and Negative logic is covered in the syllabus unit “Module-3 Logic Gates & Boolean Algebra”. A complete answer should define the concept, describe its role and steps/components, relate it to NOT, NAND, NOR, X-OR Gate, provide a practical example, and state a relevant check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as NOT; NAND; NOR; X-OR Gate. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Digital Electronics task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** logic level and pulse waveforms is covered in the syllabus unit “Module-1 Introduction”. A complete answer should define the concept, describe its role and steps/components, relate it to Digital and Analog systems, elements of digital logic, Functions of digital logic, Module-1 Introduction, provide a practical example, and state a relevant check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Digital and Analog systems; elements of digital logic; Functions of digital logic; Module-1 Introduction. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Digital Electronics task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

